    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d839">

                <pb facs="1076" n="974" sameAs="frus:frus1935v01/pg974"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">681.003/166: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Straus</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Paris</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1935-03-18T11:00:00+01:00">March 18, 1935—11
                            a.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received March 18—8:15 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>213. Reference Department’s telegram No. 92 of March 16, 3 p.m. It would seem
                    wiser to defer formal protest in connection with rights under the Algéciras
                    Treaty until results of Laval’s<anchor n="59" xml:id="d839-f1"/> appearance
                    before Senate Finance Commission on Double Taxation are known. (See my telegram
                    No. 209, March 16, 10 a.m.)<anchor n="60" xml:id="d839-f2"/> This especially
                    since Department’s views relative to Morocco are already well known to Foreign
                    Office through informal discussions.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Straus</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
